💭 A ⚪️ held in a variable, cast with 🔲, and the cast bound to a variable.
💭
💭 Expected: "made at run time, number 0", 1 and 2, and then "done".
💭 What happens: the first line is printed, and then a segmentation fault. The text is
💭 released twice: once for the variable that held it, and once for the variable the
💭 cast was bound to, which took no reference of its own. A cast that is no downcast of
💭 a class asks for its operand by reference (ASTCast::analyse), and a variable that is
💭 read by reference is not retained (ASTGetVariable::generate). A value of a
💭 protocol's type cast to its class does the same.
💭
💭 A cast that is used where it stands and never bound is fine: 📐 🍺 🔲 value 🔡❗️. So is
💭 a bound cast of what a call gives: ↪️ 🔲 🐽 dictionary 🔤key🔤❗️ 🔡 ➡️ text 🍇 … 🍉.
